{"gid":"2026_05_CIN_MIA","week":5,"away":"CIN","home":"MIA","away_col":"#FB4F14","home_col":"#2CD3C6","spread":-5.5,"total":49.5,"fav":"CIN","hpts":22,"apts":28,"kickoff":"Sun Oct 11 \u00b7 13:00","roof":"Outdoors","report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><p><strong>Ground mismatch:</strong> MIA are run-first (40% pass when leading) into a CIN front that bleeds <strong>137 rush yds/game</strong> and 0.59 RB1 rushing scores. <strong>De'Von Achane</strong> is a centerpiece.</p><p>The <strong>MIA</strong> welcome the <strong>CIN</strong> as underdogs of <strong>5.5</strong>, with the total set at <strong>49.5</strong> \u2014 a market that implies a <strong>CIN 28\u201322</strong> scoreline. Running the game forward from that baseline, we expect a contest that spends roughly <strong>40% of its snaps with CIN in front</strong>, <strong>36% within one score</strong>, and <strong>24% with CIN chasing</strong>. That distribution is the engine of everything below: game state is what turns a team's tendencies into a specific player's workload, so before we get to individuals it's worth being explicit about <em>how</em> this game is likely to be played.</p><p>A <strong>modest edge to CIN</strong>. They should lead more often than not, tilting them slightly toward the run late, while MIA leans a touch more pass-heavy to stay in it \u2014 but the effect is gentle; this isn't a script that dramatically reshapes anyone's usage.</p><h3>The Key Matchups</h3><p>MIA's outside-zone run game (35% to the edge) attacks a leaky CIN front \u2014 chunk-run upside for <strong>De'Von Achane</strong>.</p><p>Efficiency edge to CIN \u2014 a 46% success-rate offense against a MIA defense that allows 46% \u2014 points should come.</p><p><strong>Malik Willis</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 CIN allow <strong>241 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Mike Gesicki</strong> has room to work \u2014 MIA allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> has room to work \u2014 CIN allow <strong>46 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 CIN are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.47 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p>In the red zone CIN throw it (64% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><p>In the red zone MIA throw it (55% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>A modest lean to CIN \u2014 they should lead more often than not, nudging them toward the run late and giving <strong>Chase Brown</strong> a gentle game-script tailwind.</p><h3>CIN \u2014 a <em>pass-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CIN stay aggressive through the air even with a lead \u2014 they pass <strong>49%</strong> on early downs when ahead versus the league's 44%. They don't sit on the ball, which keeps their receivers live in scripts where most offenses would be running out the clock.</p><p><strong>Chase Brown</strong> (13 carries, 57 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though CIN's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Ja'Marr Chase</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 84 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><h3>MIA \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>MIA are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 MIA project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>De'Von Achane</strong>'s rushing projection (16 carries, 90 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Jalen Tolbert</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, MIA funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>4 targets for 26 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Greg Dulcich</strong> (TE, 4 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 6-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"CIN","col":"#FB4F14","players":[{"name":"Joe Burrow","pos":"QB","starter":true,"minor":false,"markets":[{"label":"Pass yards","proj":246.3,"book":null,"over_th":229.89,"under_th":256.6,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false},{"label":"Pass TDs","proj":1.89,"book":null,"over_th":1.27,"under_th":1.46,"over_am":null,"under_am":null,"pover":null,"call":null,"ev":null,"edge":null,"skew":false}],"td":{"pct":9.2,"fair":"10/1","book":nul